# modules/sast/rules/ldap.rules - docs/DESIGN.md §6.3 "ldap.rules ... LDAP
# filters from unescaped input" seed catalog: a search filter built by
# concatenation or interpolation, a distinguished name built the same way, and
# a filter value taken straight from request-derived data with nothing escaped.
#
# The three checks are three distinct code shapes, not one pattern wearing
# three names - the split modules/sast/rules/javascript.rules established for
# prototype pollution.  A filter and a distinguished name are different
# grammars with different metacharacters (RFC 4515's `( ) * \ NUL` against RFC
# 4514's `, + " \ < > ;`) and different escaping helpers, and a filter arriving
# whole from the request is a third shape that no amount of escaping inside the
# application fixes.  They share one CWE because CWE-90 covers the whole family;
# no narrower id exists for the distinguished-name half.
#
# The pack covers the LDAP clients for the three languages the shipped packs
# already cover - python-ldap, JNDI, and ldapjs.  No new language is introduced
# here.
#
# Every `files` glob is written as a plain extension pattern (`*.py`), never
# `**/*.py`.  Per rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.1.2 an unanchored glob already
# matches at any depth, whereas `**/` requires at least one directory
# component and so silently MISSES a file at the scan root - invisible in a
# fixture tree, where every path is nested by construction.
#
# SAST-LDAP-FILTER_CONCAT-01's `context-deny` is a SAME-LINE-INTENT guard and
# therefore pins `context-window: 0`, exactly as rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §10.2 and
# docs/FOUNDATION.md finding F4 require: the escaping helper belongs to the
# expression that builds the filter, so it is on that line by construction
# (`filter_format("(uid=%s)", [uid])`, `"(uid=" + escapeLDAPSearchFilter(u) +
# ")"`).  A wider window would let an unrelated escaped filter two lines away
# suppress a genuinely unescaped one, and tension 12 would then report the
# suppressed finding `fixed` rather than `new` - a false negative wearing a
# fix's clothes.  The accepted cost is the opposite direction: a value escaped
# on an EARLIER line is reported, which is a visible false positive a reviewer
# can dismiss.
#
# SAST-LDAP-DN_CONCAT-01 carries no `context-deny` at all, for the reason
# javascript.rules gives for SAST-JS-UNSAFE_MERGE-01.  Its `context-require`
# needs a window (a distinguished name is routinely assembled into a variable
# one or two lines above the call that consumes it), and `context-window` is a
# single value shared by require and deny, so a deny here could only exist at
# that same widened window - the false-negative trade §10.2 forbids.  Not
# attempting suppression is the only F4-safe choice, and the safe equivalent is
# a genuinely different shape: read the entry's distinguished name back from
# the directory rather than assembling it from a supplied value.
#
# SAST-LDAP-UNESCAPED_FILTER_INPUT-01's `context-require` exists because
# `.search(` alone is not an LDAP call - it is also String.prototype.search and
# a dozen other APIs.  Widening a require window only ever admits more lines to
# satisfy a condition that must already hold, so its worst case is an extra
# reviewable finding (§10.2); the window matches the 15 lines
# modules/sast/rules/go.rules already uses for the same kind of anchor.
#
# The boundary against modules/sast/rules/nosql.rules is the subject matter
# itself, and is asserted in both directions in tests/suites/sast.sh: no LDAP
# check may attach to a NoSQL fixture, and no NoSQL check to an LDAP one.  The
# two packs are each other's likeliest collision, because both match a query
# call taking a concatenated quoted string.  What separates them is the
# grammar each pattern anchors on - an LDAP filter's `(attr=` opener and a
# distinguished name's `attr=` RDN here, a Mongo operator or a parsed document
# there.

id: SAST-LDAP-FILTER_CONCAT-01
title: LDAP search filter built by concatenating or interpolating an unescaped value
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-90
owasp: A05:2025
pattern: \(([&|!][[:space:]]*)?\(?(uid|cn|sn|mail|givenName|sAMAccountName|userPrincipalName|objectClass|member|memberOf)=[^)]*(["'][[:space:]]*\+|\$\{|\.format\(|%[[:space:]]*[A-Za-z_(])
dialect: ere
context-deny: (escape_filter_chars|filter_format|escapeLDAPSearchFilter|filterEncode|encodeForLDAP|ldapEscape|escapeFilter)
context-window: 0
files: *.js
files: *.jsx
files: *.ts
files: *.tsx
files: *.mjs
files: *.cjs
files: *.py
files: *.java
tags: static
tags: quick
remediation: Escape every filter metacharacter in the value on the expression that builds
  the filter, using the library's own helper (python-ldap's filter_format or
  escape_filter_chars, Spring's LdapEncoder.filterEncode, ESAPI's encodeForLDAP), and keep
  the filter itself a fixed template. An unescaped parenthesis or asterisk lets the
  supplied value close the current assertion and add one of its own, turning a lookup for
  one account into a match for every account.
references: CWE-90

id: SAST-LDAP-DN_CONCAT-01
title: LDAP distinguished name built by concatenating an unescaped value
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-90
owasp: A05:2025
pattern: ["'][^"'()]*\b(cn|uid|ou|dc)[[:space:]]*=[^"'()]*["'][[:space:]]*\+
dialect: ere
context-require: \.(lookup|bind|simple_bind_s|bind_s|search|search_s|modify|modify_s|add|add_s|rename|modrdn|unbind)[[:space:]]*\(|InitialDirContext|LdapContext|SearchRequest|BindRequest|distinguishedName
context-window: 3
files: *.js
files: *.jsx
files: *.ts
files: *.tsx
files: *.mjs
files: *.cjs
files: *.py
files: *.java
tags: static
remediation: Look the entry up with an escaped search and use the distinguished name the
  directory returns, rather than assembling one from a supplied value. Where a name really
  must be built, escape each relative-distinguished-name value with the library's own
  helper (python-ldap's escape_dn_chars, JNDI's Rdn.escapeValue). An unescaped comma or
  plus sign lets the supplied value add relative components of its own and move the
  operation to a different subtree - or, on a bind, to a different identity.
references: CWE-90

id: SAST-LDAP-UNESCAPED_FILTER_INPUT-01
title: LDAP search filter taken directly from request-derived data
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-90
owasp: A05:2025
pattern: \.(search_ext_s|search_ext|search_st|search_s|searchSubtree|searchSync|search)[[:space:]]*\([^)]*(req|request)\.(body|query|params|args|form|json)
dialect: ere
context-require: (ldap|Ldap|LDAP|DirContext|SearchControls|SearchRequest)
context-window: 15
files: *.js
files: *.jsx
files: *.ts
files: *.tsx
files: *.mjs
files: *.cjs
files: *.py
files: *.java
tags: static
remediation: Keep the filter expression in the application and let the request supply only
  a value, escaped with the library's own helper before it is placed into that fixed
  template. A request that supplies the whole filter can read any attribute of any entry
  the bound account can see, and no amount of escaping applied afterwards can recover the
  structure the caller has already chosen.
references: CWE-90
